How does a university reporting application access Microsoft 365, and which records can show its activity?
 University reporting application is a software application that collects, organizes and records present information of staff members, faculties and students enrolled in university. University stores attendance, fees, marks, documents and various things in Microsoft 365 services such as OneDrive, SharePoint, Outlook, etc. There must be some controlled authorization and protected rules for any university to get access over the data that they are storing. 
For Microsoft 365 application, the university is the third-party organization, that raises a question about how Microsoft 365 software knows that the party should be trusted, this brings these steps: The first step is Registration, it's needed to be recognized who is coming with what IP address. So, Microsoft Entra ID is there for registeration ID. 
Then Permission is required for university application to get  access over data but only for when it is granted by Microsoft 365. The third step is authorization, the third-party organization gets access token by using OAuth 2.0 to verify Microsoft Entra ID, IP address of the device and permissions.
Registration ID is for identity, and Access token is a proof that ID has authorization for accessing data. When University application requests for documents saved in OneDrive from Microsoft Office 365, to establish the communication there is an interface called Microsoft Graph API, that handles all the communication from university application to the main app.
There are two major permission models under Microsoft Graph API, Delegated permissions and Application permissions. In delegate permission, application provides permission as per signed-in user and in Application permissions, application access data without user signing in automatically.
All the activities happening, whether it's storing, arrangement of data or request to access it getting permitted or denied, everything is maintained under logs. There are four types of logs; Sign-in logs, Audit logs, Graph activity logs, Unified Audit logs. Logs are digital records that shows which record can show the activity is requested by third party organization.
Sign-in log keeps record of whether user’s authentication is successful, IP address of user signing in, and User ID. Audit logs keeps record of the modifications in permissions, registrations and configurations. Graph activity logs keep record of API requests. Lastly, Unified audit logs keep track of the changes and details of the services used under Microsoft 365 software.
